NB: update squid.conf, run '-k parse', reload systemd squid service.

# On any hosts (but squid and openvpn)
1] We see the auto-generated certificate. If client uses apt, squid will not perform SSL-Bump (we illustrate using curl).

$ curl -v -x http://squid.vagrant-dummy-ops.lab:3128 --proxy-user 'squid:chocol@tepie94' --proxy-basic https://dummy-ops.dev/ -o /dev/null
***
* SSL connection using TLSv1.3 / TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384
* ALPN: server did not agree on a protocol. Uses default.
* Server certificate:
*  subject: CN=dummy-ops.dev
*  start date: Aug 27 17:07:49 2026 GMT
*  expire date: Nov 25 18:07:45 2026 GMT
*  subjectAltName: host "dummy-ops.dev" matched cert's "dummy-ops.dev"
*  issuer: C=FR; L=PARIS; ST=IDF; O=DUMMY_OPS_TEAM; OU=DUMMY_DEV_TEAM; CN=squid-mitm-ca.vagrant-dummy-ops.lab; emailAddress=shijuro@dovecot.vagrant-dummy-ops.lab
*  SSL certificate verify ok.
* using HTTP/1.x
***

$ curl -v -x http://squid.vagrant-dummy-ops.lab:3128 --proxy-user 'squid:chocol@tepie94' --proxy-basic https://deb.debian.org/ -o /dev/null
***
* SSL connection using TLSv1.3 / TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
* ALPN: server accepted h2
* Server certificate:
*  subject: CN=cdn-fastly.deb.debian.org
*  start date: Aug  9 01:03:17 2026 GMT
*  expire date: Nov  7 01:03:16 2026 GMT
*  subjectAltName: host "deb.debian.org" matched cert's "deb.debian.org"
*  issuer: C=US; O=Let's Encrypt; CN=YR2
*  SSL certificate verify ok.
} [5 bytes data]
* using HTTP/2
***


